tions that they had waited too long  to ask on august the 18th the times of  london  came out with an extraordinary call for  chamberlain to include churchill in the  government  amazingly it was endorsed by hundreds of  staff members  from every british university before the  prime minister could act  on this advice the nazis made their  aggressive  non-aggression pact with stalin and  swept into poland launching the  blitzkrieg  assault that would work so well on  unsuspecting  or ill-prepared defenders chamberlain  rested but perhaps not all that  refreshed by his  scottish holiday learned of the polish  invasion on september 1st  but true to his nature he waited until  september the third  to declare war hoping somehow  that hitler could be persuaded to recall  the troops  as if a blitzkrieg was only a rowdy  picnic  you could cancel at a moment's notice  chamberlain couldn't even make up his  mind about how to employ churchill  on september the 1st he asked him to  join the cabinet without giving him a  specific office  and waited in agonizing 24 hours before  telling churchill the big news  that he would be once again first lord  of the admiralty  it was a painful decision for  chamberlain forcing him to admit that  peace  had failed and that the country needed a  powerful hand  at the controls of what was still  britain's greatest  military force the royal navy winston is  back  the naval chiefs signaled to the fleet  or so the legend goes  this was the beginning of churchill's  vindication and everyone knew it  because he had been such an unrelenting  critic of appeasement  and of the slow pace of re-arming  britain  and because he had been told so often  that he was wrong  he could now let the unfolding events in  poland show  that he had been right all along yet no  one  not even churchill could put the case  for war in this particular time and  place  in a way that would be entirely logical  wars aren't logical and that's why there  is so much room for debating  whether this move or that move made  sense  consider for example the stunning  omission of the soviet union  from britain's plan for war was it  logical to fight germany over the  invasion of poland  and not fight russia which sent the red  army into poland on september the 17th  1939 by the end of the month stalin's  forces had overrun most of the polish  resistance  in the eastern part of the country and  had taken hundreds  of thousands of prisoners about 20 000  prisoners  mostly polish officers and a large group  of the educated elite  were killed in mass executions in the  following spring  the british excuse was that they had  promised to defend poland  against germany not russia the french  didn't have an excuse so the illogical  answer is that the soviets were given  a pass neither the french nor the  british wanted to fight  both hitler and stalin add to this the  fact that  many in the west believed that stalin's  alliance with germany was merely an  expedient ploy  it was seen as his way of buying time to  prepare  for war against his temporary partner  in crime churchill  was one of the few leaders in the west  who was  honest enough to say precisely why  hitler  not stalin was being held to account for  the attack on poland  his argument wasn't logical but simply  realistic  in his first radio speech of the war  churchill said  russia has pursued a cold policy  of self-interest clearly necessary for  the safety of russia  against the nazi menace i cannot  forecast to you  the action of russia it is a riddle  wrapped in a mystery  inside an enigma but perhaps there is a  key  that key is russian national interest  in hitler churchill saw a maniac  with whom not even friends could reason  he was a danger to everyone in the  soviets he saw a tyrannical state  that nevertheless kept its national  interests in mind  and might yield to pressures that  recognized the importance  of that self-interest he was already  looking for an  ally against hitler on an eastern front  that did not yet exist  just as he was already looking two years  before pearl harbor  for an ally in america to help him on  the western front  he could see far enough ahead to realize  that britain and france  could not carry the war deep into  central europe  and eradicate on their own the nazi  menace  as he called it without help from at  least one  other major power  interestingly his radio remarks about  russia  also included a fascinating connection  that he wanted to draw  between a landmark war against slavery  and the current fight against nazism  a connection in which he hoped america  would soon see the chance  for forging a common front against  hitler  this is what he said of all the wars  that men have fought  in their hard pilgrimage none was more  noble  than the great civil war in america  nearly 80 years ago  both sides fought with high conviction  and the war was long and hard  all the heroism of the south could not  redeem their cause  from the stain of slavery just as all  the courage and skill  which the germans always show in war  will not  free them from the reproach of nazism  with its intolerance and brutality  some americans in the old south weren't  pleased by this comparison and churchill  did worry  that he had gone too far but his target  audience  really an audience of one franklin  roosevelt reported that he was very  pleased  by the remarks even at this very  early stage of the war when he was only  a newly minted member of the war cabinet  churchill was using his knowledge of  history and strategy  to cast his net far and wide to draw in  the necessary allies  to make sure that hitler was crushed  before he had the chance to destroy  europe it wouldn't be pretty it wouldn't  always be logical  but it was a strategy that would work  and it began with churchill's practical  as opposed to theoretical understanding  of how wars  are won or lost  we can fault churchill for individual  episodes in the war when judgment failed  him  but not on the larger question of  victory where his eye was always firmly  fixed  so many wars start with no plan for  victory no firm purpose  or clear aim through all the fogs of war  that inevitably  rolled through the european theater of  conflict churchill's aim  was always to surround hitler and  destroy nazism  at its root that is his central and  enduring legacy  but mistakes were made especially in the  early months of the fighting  when britain was still scrambling to  pull itself  into a war footing and when the french  were still hunkered behind the maginot  line hoping  that the germans wouldn't be tempted to  test its  supposed invulnerability though  chamberlain announced  that britain was at war in september he  organized no major offensive against  germany  partly because he wanted to respect the  neutrality of belgium  and hold british troops back in france  to await the expected nazi invasion  only then would the allies move forward  to engage with the enemy  the problem was that the british forces  on the ground were never larger than 10  percent of the french army  in effect they were a token force not  especially well equipped or well trained  if the french didn't take the lead the  british were in no position  to move on their own  waiting for your enemy to make the first  big move is  never a good strategy and as the months  of the so-called  phony war dragged into 1940 the british  public grew  increasingly frustrated with  chamberlain's caution  what they did not know is that since  september  churchill had been urging the war  cabinet to undertake a risky assault on  the port of narvik in northern norway  where vital iron ore for the german  steel industry  was being shipped out along the coast in  the safety  of neutral waters it's debatable whether  stopping the shipping would deal germany  the severe blow that  church will imagine but he was  determined to make chamberlain  take some decisive action before as he  expected  the germans themselves occupied norway  to safeguard  their supplies of iron ore  again the question of the laws of  neutrality gave chamberlain pause  if the british were not willing to enter  belgium why should they go into norway  cabinet debates over this and other  questions entangled the prime minister  and a major assault was put off for so  long  that when it did come in april it was  far too late and too disorganized  the germans staged their own invasion of  norway at about the same time  and with better equipment and better air  cover they easily prevailed  leading to a disastrous defeat for the  british who were forced to retreat  it looked for a moment as though  churchill would have his return  to the admiralty abruptly ended by a  major tragedy  like the one in the dardanelles in 1915.  again  he had made a bold suggestion and  watched his plans undermined by delays  and confusion as in the case of the  dardanelles  he was trying to strike a surprise blow  outside of the central theater of war  his main fault was not so much in the  execution of the plan  where so many others had a hand in  making a mess of it but in pushing the  idea  in the first place chamberlain's  indecisiveness  caused churchill to take his eye off the  only thing that really mattered  in 1939-1940 stopping  hitler from overrunning france  not only did belgium neutral or not need  to be fortified against the expected  attack  but also that vulnerable spot at the  ardan forest  where the maginot defenses ran out and  needed  major reinforcement the french were so  sure of its insignificance that they  left it defended by some of their oldest  and least capable troops  of course this is exactly where one  german army  burst through in may 1940 while another  moved into belgium it was a brilliant  ploy french and british troops  raced north to save belgium and found  that the german forces coming through  the yard and  were sweeping through france right  behind them trapping them  and leaving only one little enclave for  escape  the channel port in france near the  belgian border  dunkirk by the spring of 1940  there were more than 300 000 british  troops in france  whose own army numbered in the millions  correctly deployed these forces should  have been able to fight the germans  for a lot longer than six weeks but  that's all the time it took  for the enemy to win in a route perhaps  more than any other british leader  churchill wanted to keep the troops  fighting alongside the french when  hitler invaded  but years of neglect had given britain a  land force ill-equipped  to stop the germans and the panic among  french leaders  soon made it clear that they lacked the  will to save their  own country  norway was a sideshow yet it is also  true that the british  expeditionary force in france could not  alone have stopped germany  at the ardan or elsewhere without more  men  and better arms especially tanks and  anti-tank weapons  the truth is that as first lord of the  admiralty churchill could do little to  reverse  the perilous position of the outnumbered  and outgunned british forces  and its complacent french partner wedded  to a bad strategy of defensive war  and plagued from the start by frankly a  defeatist  attitude the british public  understood in 1940 that churchill had  been in the cabinet  for only a few months and that his  leader chamberlain  had played a prominent part in  government for years  and that this same prime minister was  now painfully exposed as a national  failure  whose sheet of paper heralding an age of  peace  had turned out to be an empty promise  it wasn't luck or subterfuge that saved  churchill from another dardanelle  disaster  in the case of norway in 1915 he had  been the target of a lot of  unhappy politicians who yearned for his  downfall  in 1940 it was chamberlain who found  himself  in this position  however much he misjudged the situation  in norway  churchill was at least trying to find a  way to engage the germans in war  and to win despite his failings the  norway campaign  resulted in the sinking of several  german ships causing serious damage to  hitler's  navy and hampering its ability to launch  an invasion  of the english coast later that year  after  so many fruitless years of talk about  peace agreements  and arms reductions the sheer brutality  of the nazi war machine made the british  people  want to see a bit of fighting spirit in  their leaders  churchill had that chamberlain did not  and the public turned on him holding him  as head of the government to be the one  most responsible  for the government's failures on may 8  1940 just two days before the germans  invaded holland and belgium  churchill's old rival from the first  days of the  first world war the former prime  minister david lord george  now a white-haired political veteran of  77  stood up in the house of commons and  made an extraordinary speech  confessing to the whole house that he  felt he had been misled  by stanley baldwin in the big questions  of war and peace that churchill had been  right  on those matters all along and that  chamberlain had  failed to lead the country now that it  was at war  the speech was a political death blow  for the prime minister  here is what lord george said for three  or four years i thought to myself that  the facts with regard to germany  were exaggerated by the first lord  meaning churchill  because the then prime minister in other  words baldwin  said that they were not true the first  lord was right about it then  came the war the tempo was hardly  speeded up  there was the same leisureliness and  inefficiency  nobody is satisfied the whole world  knows that  and here we are in the worst strategic  position in which this country  has ever been placed chamberlain not  only had to suffer through this  humiliating speech but also to exit the  house that day  with members shouting at his back go go  go go the atmosphere was so full of  hostility  toward chamberlain that one of his  remaining allies on the floor thought  that the prime minister  might be physically attacked what  winchen churchill  went through in 1915 when he was the  scapegoat  for war going badly was nothing compared  to this  very public rejection of a prime  minister  whose armies were about to come under  attack in france  churchill graciously attempted in the  debates to take some of the blame on  himself  but lord george said to the house that  the first lord of the admiralty  didn't need to take the flack intended  for others as he put it  offensively churchill must not allow  himself to be converted into an air raid  shelter  to keep the splinters from hitting his  colleagues  chamberlain should have realized at this  point that he was finished  his resignation would be the price  exacted not only for the actions in  norway  but also for the inaction everywhere  else  vainly however he tried to hang on  pleading with the labor party to support  him  in a new government of national unity it  was only when  they refused that he accepted his fate  his half-spoken wish was that churchill  would not succeed him yet he was  thwarted in that  as well calling both churchill and lord  halifax to a meeting  he asked halifax whether he would be  willing to lead a new government  halifax not only declined the offer but  also added  winston would be a better choice that  sealed the matter  chamberlain resigned and on the evening  of may the 10th 1940  king george vi took chamberlain's advice  and asked winston churchill to form a  new government  the chamberlain dynasty going all the  way back to joe chamberlain birmingham  was finished and churchill who had  gotten the better of joe  four decades earlier now replaced  neville as prime minister  he was 65. europe was in flames  and he was about to lead britain in the  greatest fight  of its life even as the misadventure in  norway was still being played out  churchill within only a month of taking  office  would find himself facing one of the  most serious challenges  any prime minister had ever seen so  rapidly  with the french fall before the german  advance that over a quarter of a million  men  mostly british would find themselves  trapped between the sea  and the nazis on the beaches of dunkirk  faced was such a disaster coming so  close on the heels of the retreat from  norway  a different kind of prime minister might  have sued for peace  but the old war horse who had taken the  job had the advantage of experience that  went back to his days as a prisoner  in the boer war an officer in the  trenches of the first world war  and more recently a political cassandra  whose  unheated warnings were now being played  out so  dangerously close to london itself  how did winston churchill change the  world  join me for our next lecture and we will  try to count the ways  at the end of the road that led to  [Music]  dunkirk  [Music]  you
 

